            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 14,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Do we need a provision for moving an existing file if one exists already?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 14,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Thinking how we could get into this situation. So we start with the old code and move to the new code. /etc is moved to /var. Then they downgrade back to old code. Now we have the file in /etc and /var. They then update to new code again and there is a situation where we would just blindly move the /etc and copy over the /var. \n\nI think it would be best to always assume /etc is the \"good\" file as that's the upgrade path so blindly moving it to /var makes sense to me. We just don't want to break things in a downgrade path. But I can document that fact in the commit message and provide a warning log if there is a file in /var before copying the file from /etc."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 14,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Yep, you handled this correctly.  I missed it in my review."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Overall in support of this."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "inc/utils.hpp",
                    "line": 49,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ivan Mikhaylov",
                        "email": "fr0st61te@gmail.com",
                        "username": "fr0st61te"
                    },
                    "message": "isn't it /var/run/service-config-manager or /var/tmp/service-config-manager directory?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "inc/utils.hpp",
                    "line": 49,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "No, this is meant to be a persistent file, so it goes where the rest of the BMC persistent data goes in /var/lib/<repo name>/"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/main.cpp",
                    "line": 34,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ivan Mikhaylov",
                        "email": "fr0st61te@gmail.com",
                        "username": "fr0st61te"
                    },
                    "message": "if it's just for track systemd services, maybe it shouldn't be at /etc at all? And keeping /etc part is obsolete? It just puzzles users who using service which contains config at /etc and it isn't config but tracker."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/main.cpp",
                    "line": 34,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm not sure I'm fully understanding you here. I agree it shouldn't be in /etc as I tried to explain in my commit message. This commit moves the file to /var and stops using /etc. Please reopen this comment if I didn't explain it well enough."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/main.cpp",
                    "line": 34,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ivan Mikhaylov",
                        "email": "fr0st61te@gmail.com",
                        "username": "fr0st61te"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm saying that when you have logic like:\n\nmoving srvcfg-mgr.json from /etc to /var\nplus logic about existence of persistence storage at /var/lib/service-config-manager\n\nWhy it shouldn't be just changing of the place without any logic of moving files? So, like:\n\ncreate /var/lib/service-config-manager dir if it doesn't exist\nput json here\n\nor you care about backward compatibility with old one style to make smooth transition to new way of work?"
                }
            ],
